/**

@page tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_setup Setup of ExaGRyPE

The ExaGRyPE code is organised by a main Python script (i.e., \c ccz4.py), which we refer to as the Python API. By calling this script, Peano will automatically create the project and generate the actual C++ code according to the user's setup, and then compile the final executable. We integrate most of the tunable features and settings in the API script, so users can easily control the behaviour of their simulations by modifying the single script and the corresponding calling command. There is also some setup in the C++ files such as \c CCZ4.cpp or \c src/exagrype/initialvalues/InitialValues.cpp .

Below, we provide a brief introduction to the important setup options provided by ExaGRyPE. One can find useful information by typing \c --help for an explanation of the parameters that can be controlled directly by the calling command. Most of them should be self-explanatory by name as well.

# Solvers

The solver, which is responsible for the exact numerical scheme that updates cells, can be selected by specifying the \c args.implementation arguments. There are multiple default solver options available to users, and for vacuum simulations in ExaGRyPE, the \c fd4-rk1-dsl solver is recommended. This solver stands for a fourth-order finite differences + Euler time integration with adaptive time stepping that adjusts the step size according to the system eigenvalue. This solver balances accuracy and performance; most of the results in the release paper of ExaGRyPE are produced with this solver. A higher-order RK scheme gives better time accuracy but with a higher computational burden. The \c -dsl suffix indicates a solver that was generated by DSL (domain-specific language).

We also recommend using the second-order formulation (note that this is not the order of FD accuracy) by adding the \c -so flag to your calling command, as the first-order formulation can give some unexpected fluctuation behaviour in static scenarios such as the single Schwarzschild black hole (see next section).

The other solvers are not recommended for vacuum scenario simulations, including the outdated ones that are no longer maintained (e.g., with suffix \c -adaptive) or the finite volume type solver that yields high diffusion.

# Formulations

ExaGRyPE builds upon the ExaHyPE engine, which only accepts the first-order formulation, i.e., it requires the PDE system to contain only first-order spatial derivatives in its physical terms. The equations of numerical relativity, on the other hand, contain second-order spatial derivatives. To fit them into the formulation of ExaHyPE, ExaGRyPE introduces auxiliary variables, which represent the first-order derivatives of the original evolving variables (called primary variables). The second-order spatial derivatives in the equations can thus be converted into first-order spatial derivatives of the auxiliary variables, reducing the system to the first-order formulation.

The next step is to determine the evolution method of those auxiliary variables. There are two approaches:
1. We use the commutativity of the temporal and spatial derivatives to obtain the evolution equations of the auxiliary variables, making them updated as independent variables. This is what we call the first-order formulation (FO) of ExaGRyPE.
2. We use a finite difference approximation to calculate those auxiliary variables using their definition (i.e., calculate the first-order spatial derivatives of the primaries and assign them to the auxiliaries) before every time step. This is more complicated compared to the FO formulation, but allows us to solve the system in the second-order formulation (SO). In this case, we have access to the actual second-order spatial derivatives of the primaries, which are calculated as the first-order spatial derivatives of their first-order spatial derivatives (the auxiliaries) in the pre-time-stepping step.

ExaGRyPE now supports both formulations for flexibility, and we find their behaviour varies according to the physical scenario. For a single Schwarzschild black hole spacetime, please use the SO formulation as the FO formulation leads to a growing fluctuation near the puncture and is not stable in the long term.

@image html tutorials/exahype2/exagrype/formulations.svg

# Scenarios

The main testing scenarios in ExaGRyPE are given on the previous page, which include the gauge wave, single black hole (SBH), and rotating binary black holes (BBHs). While different scenarios can be switched directly via the calling command, the exact parameters (e.g., the amplitude of the gauge wave, the mass of the black holes) need to be set in the corresponding C++ file (\c src/exagrype/initialvalues/InitialValues.cpp for the gauge wave, \c CCZ4.cpp for the black holes). We recommend users always start with the gauge wave benchmark, as it serves as a good starting point for familiarising themselves with how the code is organised. For black hole spacetimes, we import the \c TwoPuncture module from EinsteinToolkit as an external library. If users plan to import their own external library for the initial condition, the binding code for the TwoPuncture module in the \c External directory should serve as a useful reference, and see also \ref tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_advanced.

## Black Hole Parameters Setup

ExaGRyPE uses the external library \c TwoPunctures from EinsteinToolkit to generate initial conditions for black hole spacetimes, using the puncture method. In the application's \c CCZ4.cpp you will find the following function:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
void applications::exahype2::ccz4::InitialCondtions::PrepareTPdata(TP::TwoPunctures* tp, ...){
    if (....) {  //SBH
    tp->par_b=1.0;
    tp->center_offset[0]=-1.0; tp->center_offset[1]=0.0; tp->center_offset[2]=0.0;
    tp->target_M_plus=1.0;//adm mass
    tp->par_P_plus[0]=0.0; tp->par_P_plus[1]=0.0; tp->par_P_plus[2]=0.0;//linear momentum
    tp->par_S_plus[0]=0.0; tp->par_S_plus[1]=0.0; tp->par_S_plus[2]=0.0;//spin
    tp->target_M_minus=0.0;//adm mass
    tp->par_P_minus[0]=0.0; tp->par_P_minus[1]=0.0; tp->par_P_minus[2]=0.0;//linear momentum
    tp->par_S_minus[0]=0.0; tp->par_S_minus[1]=0.0; tp->par_S_minus[2]=0.0; //spin    
    tp->grid_setup_method="evaluation"; //evaluation or Taylor expansion
    tp->TP_epsilon=1e-6;}
......
    if (....) {  //BBHs
    {tp->par_b=5.0;
    tp->center_offset[0]=0.0; tp->center_offset[1]=0.0; tp->center_offset[2]=0.0;
    tp->give_bare_mass=true;//use puncture mass instead of adm mass
    tp->par_m_plus=0.48595; tp->par_m_minus=0.48595;
    //tp->target_M_plus=999;//adm mass, not used
    tp->par_P_plus[0]=0.0; tp->par_P_plus[1]=0.095433; tp->par_P_plus[2]=0.0;//linear momentum
    tp->par_S_plus[0]=0.0; tp->par_S_plus[1]=0.0; tp->par_S_plus[2]=0.0;//spin
    //tp->target_M_minus=999;//adm mass, not used
    tp->par_P_minus[0]=0.0; tp->par_P_minus[1]=-0.095433; tp->par_P_minus[2]=0.0;//linear momentum
    tp->par_S_minus[0]=0.0; tp->par_S_minus[1]=0.0; tp->par_S_minus[2]=0.0; //spin    
    tp->grid_setup_method="evaluation"; //evaluation or Taylor expansion
    tp->TP_epsilon=1e-6;}
......
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

where you can modify the physical parameters (initial distance between BHs, mass, spins, etc.) for the initial condition. Note that you need to rerun the Python API script after modifying the parameters here. Currently, we only support hard-coded parameter setup at the compilation stage, i.e., no on-the-fly parameter setup.

