# AMR Control

In \c CCZ4.cpp, we provide a function \c refinementCriterion() to control the AMR pattern:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
::exahype2::RefinementCommand applications::exahype2::ccz4::CCZ4::refinementCriterion(
  const double * __restrict__ Q,
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>& volumeCentre,
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>& volumeH,
  double t
) {
  ......
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This function iterates through the grid volumes and reads in the coordinates, size, and solution array in each volume. It then outputs the \c RefinementCommand, including \c keep, \c refine, and \c erase (which are used in dynamic AMR, not fully tested yet). By specifying the function output using the information it reads in, users can control the AMR pattern they need. For example, the following function content:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
::exahype2::RefinementCommand result = ::exahype2::RefinementCommand::Keep;
if (SomeConditionFlag){ 
  constexpr int NumberOfRefinementLayers = 2;
  double Radius[NumberOfRefinementLayers] = {3.0, 1.5};
  double MaxH[NumberOfRefinementLayers]   = {0.15,0.04};
  result = ::exahype2::RefinementCommand::Keep;
  for (int i=0; i<NumberOfRefinementLayers; i++) {
    if (tarch::la::norm2(volumeX)<Radius[i] and tarch::la::max(meshCellH)>MaxH[i]) {
     result=::exahype2::RefinementCommand::Refine;
    }
  }
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

instructs the code to refine to the next finer level when \f$ r<3 \f$, then refine again to the finest level when \f$ r<1.5 \f$ (the actual refinement radius will be larger than specified due to Peano's refinement strategy). This AMR pattern is used in the single black hole benchmark in this tutorial.

## Interpolation and Restriction

ExaGRyPE provides several strategies for how solutions along the AMR boundary (resolution transition boundary) are interpolated (restricted), with different accuracy and computational burden. The updated strategy \c matrix is much faster compared to the legacy ones while maintaining linear accuracy. The high-order strategy \c second-order is slightly more expensive but yields a much better transition along the boundary; we therefore recommend enabling this one for simulations involving black holes (SBH or BBHs cases). For more information on the strategies, one can refer to <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15814">this paper</a>.

# Checkpoint and Restarting

See \ref page_exahype_checkpointing


*/